<tr><td align=3D"left" class=3D"text" style=3D"background:#FFFFFF;font-size=
:0px;padding:0 16px;word-break:break-word;"><div style=3D"font-family:Georg=
ia, serif;font-size:16px;font-weight:400;line-height:22px;text-align:left;c=
olor:#121212;">
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Next spring, when the US Soccer Hall of Fa=
me welcomes its newest class, there will be pomp and circumstance. The hall=
, which sits in a corner of Toyota Stadium, home of Major League Soccer=E2=
=80=99s FC Dallas, will feature the enshrineds=E2=80=99 signature red jacke=
ts. There will be glad-handing and speeches from players and coaches, misty=
-eyed reflections on their upbringings and their careers.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Many involved will be American-born soccer=
 legends, of course. Yet every once in a while, a player whose footprint ex=
tends well beyond the borders of the United States graces the stage. Such m=
ay be the case next year when the hall potentially welcomes English legend =
David Beckham into the fold.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Some readers abroad =E2=80=93 and certainl=
y plenty of readers here in the States =E2=80=93 will wonder why an ostensi=
bly <em>American</em> Hall of Fame would admit non-Americans, something it=
=E2=80=99s done since its inception. The answer lies in the nature of the A=
merican game itself. Even our earliest heroes =E2=80=93 Joe Gaetjens and th=
e like =E2=80=93 were immigrants, with Gaetjens scoring his famed goal agai=
nst England in the 1950 World Cup when he wasn=E2=80=99t even a US citizen =
(he never ended up becoming one).</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Advance through the decades and big-name, =
American-born heroes do start to emerge, but they are still outpaced by tho=
se from abroad. The history of the North American Soccer League is replete =
with foreign-born members of the country=E2=80=99s hall of fame such as Pel=
=C3=A9, Franz Beckenbauer and Carlos Alberto. Even the Major <em>Indoor </e=
m>Soccer League got its due eventually: just last year, Steve Zungul, the =
=E2=80=9Clord of all indoors=E2=80=9D, was inducted. Tatu, another foreign-=
born legend of the arena, seems likely to go in this year.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">All of which is to say, there=E2=80=99s pr=
ecedent for this. If you made a sizable contribution to soccer in the Unite=
d States, you=E2=80=99re eligible =E2=80=93 period. It=E2=80=99s the way it=
 should be.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">By that standard, there=E2=80=99s no doubt=
ing Beckham=E2=80=99s credentials. Perhaps the most direct analog for his c=
ontributions is the career of Pel=C3=A9, who arrived stateside in 1977 and =
transformed the landscape of the game in this country. Like Beckham=E2=80=
=99s stay with the LA Galaxy, the Brazilian=E2=80=99s playing career was su=
ccessful. He won a league championship in 1977 and was wildly productive th=
roughout the entirety of his stay in the US. He did every interview, signed=
 every autograph, more or less, and went on to found a soccer camp in New J=
ersey that produced thousands of soccer-mad children, more than one of whom=
 went on to feature for the US men=E2=80=99s national team. He remained a p=
resence for decades after his retirement.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Still, after all of that, professional soc=
cer in the US more or less collapsed within seven years of his departure.</=
p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Beckham=E2=80=99s playing career with the =
Galaxy (2007-2012) was in some ways more successful than Pel=C3=A9=E2=80=99=
s. Much has been made about his tumultuous arrival in the US, so expertly t=
old by the late, great Grant Wahl in his book The Beckham Experiment<em>. <=
/em>After the smoke cleared, though, Beckham was a mainstay for the Galaxy,=
 working alongside Landon Donovan (a Hall of Famer himself) to lead the clu=
b to a pair of league titles and two Supporters=E2=80=99 Shields.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">His true effect, though, came at a league =
level. Major League Soccer created an entirely new roster mechanism =E2=80=
=93 the designated player rule =E2=80=93 to accommodate Beckham=E2=80=99s a=
rrival, a guideline that fundamentally changed its reality. Like Pel=C3=A9,=
 scores of uninitiated fans filled MLS stadiums to see Beckham. And just as=
 the Brazilian did, Beckham stayed well beyond the end of his playing days,=
 eventually founding Lionel Messi=E2=80=99s Inter Miami, the current standa=
rd bearer of the league.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">The Hall of Fame inducts candidates in thr=
ee categories: player, veteran and builder, the last of which is reserved f=
or those whose work was primarily off the field. Beckham=E2=80=99s inclusio=
n this year comes in the veteran category, where he=E2=80=99s nominated alo=
ngside nine other deserving candidates. The presence of his name has been t=
he subject of much debate among the hall=E2=80=99s voters, of which I am on=
e.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">Arguments against his inclusion center on =
the fact that, in several years, Beckham=E2=80=99s name seems likely to com=
e up on a list of =E2=80=9Cbuilder=E2=80=9D nominees. Certainly, helping to=
 found Inter Miami, and luring the greatest player in history to play there=
, is worth considering by that standard. So if he stands a good chance of m=
aking it in that way, then why not save this spot for someone else?</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">To me, though, voting him in now is an eas=
y decision =E2=80=93 why wait? Beckham=E2=80=99s playing career alone stack=
s up favorably against many members of the hall. And while he is unquestion=
ably a =E2=80=9Cbuilder=E2=80=9D, so much of that work was simultaneous wit=
h his career as a player.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">His mere presence on field legitimized a s=
till fledgeling league and led to a decade-long boom that saw it nearly dou=
ble in size. This, of course, is not solely Beckham=E2=80=99s doing =E2=80=
=93 far from it =E2=80=93 but I could easily make an argument that Beckham=
=E2=80=99s influence on American soccer is as big as Pel=C3=A9=E2=80=99s =
=E2=80=93 and Pel=C3=A9, of course, has a place in the hall.</p>
<p style=3D"margin: 0 0 16px 0;">So, English-born Beckham will be getting o=
ne of my votes for induction. In truth, three of my five selections for ind=
uction (Beckham, Tatu, Ken Cooper Sr, Tiffany Roberts and Clint Mathis) are=
 foreign-born. That feels truly American to me.</p>
